Q: How to dispose RestClient from RestSharp A: <p>Latest version of RestClient support dispose method. Use <code>using</code> block or call <code>client.Dispose()</code> in finally block. you can also pass httpclient during initialization for restclient and maintain as per your needs.</p>
<p>Update</p>
<p>source link
<a href="https://github.com/restsharp/RestSharp/blob/dev/src/RestSharp/IRestClient.cs#L20" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/restsharp/RestSharp/blob/dev/src/RestSharp/IRestClient.cs#L20</a></p>
<p>Example</p>
<pre><code>public void DisposeTest()
{
    var client = new RestClient(&quot;https://fakerestapi.azurewebsites.net/&quot;);
    var response=client.Execute(new RestRequest(&quot;/api/v1/Activities&quot;, Method.Get));
    Console.WriteLine(response.Content);
    client.Dispose();
}
`
</code></pre>

Q: Windows/php pclose and popen issue A: <pre><code>$handle = popen("start /B ". $cmd, "r");  
if ($handle === FALSE) {
   die("Unable to execute $cmd");
}
pclose($handle);
</code></pre>

<p>popen returns false if there was a problem, which you blindly pass to pclose, hence the second error.</p>

<p>As for the first error, check that PHP is in your environment's path - you may need to specify an absolute path to php.exe.</p>

Q: How should I partition a memory region returned by mmap() into allocator blocks? A: <blockquote>
<p>For example, is the correct approach to locate the next block header using byte-wise pointer arithmetic</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's one way to do it.  The question doesn't provide enough details for us to validate the specific formula it presents.</p>
<p>But if you plan for your partitions to be of consistent size, then it would be simpler to model the overall region as an array of partitions, perhaps something like this:</p>
<pre><code>struct free_list {
    size_t size;
    // ...
    // no next needed
};

struct partition {
    struct free_list free_list;
    // ... other per-partition metadata ...
    union {
        max_align_t _for_alignment;
        unsigned char bytes[SIZE_OF_DATA];
    };
};

// Supposing you arrange for sizeof(struct partition) to be 1024 * 1024:
#define NUM_PARTITIONS 10u
#define HEAP_SIZE (NUM_PARTITIONS * sizeof(struct partition))

static struct partition *partitions;
// For convenience:
#define FREE_LIST_HEAD ((struct free_list *) partitions)
</code></pre>
<p>Then:</p>
<pre><code>        void *raw = mmap(NULL, HEAP_SIZE,
                         PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                         MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS,
                         -1, 0);

        if (raw == MAP_FAILED)
            return;

        partitions = raw;
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i &lt; NUM_PARTITIONS; i++) {
            partitions[i].free_list.size = sizeof(struct partition)
                    - offsetof(struct partition, bytes));
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Now, C array indexing does most of the messy address arithmetic automatically, as demonstrated by the initialization loop above.</p>
<p>That assumes there is only per-partition metadata stored inside the region, not any whole-region metadata, but it could be adjusted to make room for whole-region metadata inside the region.</p>

Q: Serialize and deserialize property with dynamically assigned property name based on a type given in an attribute using System.Text.Json A: <p>There are two problems, in this and previous questions. None of them is about JSON.</p>
<ol>
<li>The <code>xsd.exe</code> or <code>svcutil.exe</code> tool used to create the &quot;broader&quot; class couldn't handle the XML file's type of polymorphism. It's possible to specify that an attribute can be one of several types that have no inheritance relation to each other. A better code generator would use eg a marker interface to combine all together.</li>
<li>You want to <em>change</em> the class shape and instead of eg serializing a <code>Request</code> element with different types, you want to generate separate elements per type.</li>
</ol>
<p>And 3, you want to do that in the serializer. Which means duplicating your work every time you need to use a new serializer. In any case, the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/converters-how-to" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Custom Converter docs</a> show something similar in the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/converters-how-to#deserialize-inferred-types-to-object-properties" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Deserialize inferred types to object properties</a></p>
<p>An easier way to handle this would be to tag the request types with a marker interface, eg <code>IRequest</code> :</p>
<pre><code>public interface IRequestType {}
...
public ReportRequestType :IRequestType
{
...
}

...
public TransmitRequestType:IRequestType
{
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can use partial classes for this so you don't have to modify the code-generated types.</p>
<p>After that, you can add a property to the &quot;broader&quot; class that casts to <code>IRequest</code>. You can do that in a partial class as well.</p>
<pre><code>public IRequest Request=&gt;(IRequest)Item;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/polymorphism" rel="nofollow noreferrer">System.Text.Json supports polymorphism</a> but you may have to add attributes to control the actual output.</p>
<p>As for the second problem, you can solve it <em>without</em> using a custom serializer by adding one property per type and preventing serialization of empty properties :</p>
<pre><code>public ReportRequestType? ReportRequest =&gt;Item as ReportRequestType;
...
</code></pre>
<p><code>as</code> returns <code>Null</code> if <code>Item</code> isn't a <code>ReportRequestType</code></p>
<p>Another option could be to write a custom JsonConverter, as <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/converters-how-to#deserialize-inferred-types-to-object-properties" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the docs show</a>. I'm not going to provide the full code - the question's incomplete code means I'd have to invent a <em>lot</em> of types. Again, using marker interfaces makes things easier :</p>
<pre><code>public class RequestJsonConverter : JsonConverter&lt;object&gt;
{
    public override bool CanConvert(Type typeToConvert){
    {
        return typeof(IRequest).IsAssignableFrom(typeof(typeToConvert));
        //otherwise something like
        // return typeToConvert==typeof(ReportRequestType) || ...;
    }

    public override object Read(
        ref Utf8JsonReader reader,
        Type typeToConvert,
        JsonSerializerOptions options) =&gt;return null;

    public override void Write(
        Utf8JsonWriter writer,
        IReport reportValue,
        JsonSerializerOptions options) {

            //Untested
            var actualType=reportValue.GetType();                                
            writer.WriteStartObject();
            writer.WritePropertyName(actualType.Name);
            JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, reportValue, actualType, options);
            writer.WriteEndObject();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As the docs show later on, that converter can be registered in the JsonSerializerOptions :</p>
<pre><code>        var options = new JsonSerializerOptions();
        options.WriteIndented = true;
        options.Converters.Add(new RequestJsonConverter ());
</code></pre>
